  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${x}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+200{x}^{2}+10\\,000 & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 100\\cdot ({x}^{2})+{100}^{2} \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+100)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+100 & ={x}^{2}-(-100) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(100{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{(10i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x+10i)(x-10i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+200{x}^{2}+10\\,000 & ={({x}^{2}+100)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((x+10i)(x-10i))}^{2} \\\\  & =(x+10i)^{2}(x-10i)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
